# Environment variables.
#
# Copy this file to .env.local and put real values in that copy:
#
#     cp .env.example .env.local
#
# .env.local is git-ignored (see the .env*.local rule in .gitignore). THIS file
# is committed, so every value below is a dummy and never a real credential.
#
# A missing variable must fail loudly rather than degrade silently, so add a key
# here in the same change that starts reading it — this file is the list of what
# the project needs, and it is only useful if it is complete.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database — required, the only one wired up today
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:PORT/DATABASE
#
# The local database is art_studio. The role and password below are dummies —
# replace them in .env.local with whichever role you created in pgAdmin.
#
# Neon later appends ?sslmode=require; node-postgres reads sslmode out of the
# connection string itself, so moving between local and Neon is a change to
# this value and to nothing in the code.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://orit_app:change-me@localhost:5432/art_studio

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Admin session — required, signs the session cookie
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signs the artist's httpOnly session cookie. A long random string, generated
# per environment. Never shared between local and production.
ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=replace-with-a-long-random-string

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Translation — optional, read by lib/translate.js
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google's Gemini API, which the admin form uses to fill an English title and
# description in from the Hebrew the artist actually writes. She approves or
# edits every one before the work can be saved.
#
# The key is read on the server and never reaches the browser: the form asks
# /admin/api/translate and that handler calls the provider.
#
# Optional on purpose. With no key the feature is simply absent and both English
# fields are typed by hand, which is what they were before it existed.
#
# Gemini rather than DeepL because DeepL stopped selling the free plan. Gemini's
# free tier needs no billing account. One key, one host, and the model is pinned
# in lib/translate.js rather than named here.
GEMINI_API_KEY=AIzaSyExampleKeyNotARealOne0000000000000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Site URL — required in production, read by lib/site-url.js
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Absolute base for canonical URLs, hreflang pairs, the sitemap and share
# images. Those are absolute by definition, so they cannot be built from a
# relative path.
#
# Unset, the build FAILS in production rather than guessing. A wrong base does
# not break a page: it publishes a canonical URL pointing somewhere else and an
# hreflang pair that does not resolve, and nobody finds out for weeks. On a
# laptop it falls back to the dev server's own address.
#
# No trailing slash, and it needs the scheme.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Image host — optional, read by lib/image-host.js
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where the artist's own photographs are uploaded and served from. The sixteen
# seeded paintings still come out of public/paintings; anything she attaches in
# the admin goes here, and both render through the same components.
#
# All three are needed together, and all three are server-side. The browser
# uploads the file straight to the host — a Server Action's request body is
# capped at 1MB and a photograph is several times that — but it does so with a
# signature this application produces per upload. The secret never leaves the
# server, and there is deliberately no unsigned upload preset: that would be a
# public write endpoint into her media library, named in the page source.
#
# None of them is needed to *serve* an image that has already been uploaded:
# delivery runs off the URL stored in the database, through lib/image-loader.js,
# which asks the host for each size. These three are for uploading.
#
# Optional in the same way the translation key is: with no credentials the work
# form shows the notice it showed before uploading existed, rather than offering
# a button that fails. Set them and the panel appears.
CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=example-cloud
CLOUDINARY_API_KEY=000000000000000
CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET=example-api-secret

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Payments — Hyp Pay. Settled, and nothing reads these yet
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hyp Pay on the yaad-sarig platform, docs at developers.hyp.co.il. The buyer is
# sent to Hyp's own hosted payment page, so no card detail ever reaches this
# server or this repository.
#
# All three belong to ONE terminal and are read together, server-side only. The
# terminal number is the account — "Masof" in their documentation; the API key
# and API password authenticate our server-to-server calls, above all the VERIFY
# call that decides whether a payment really happened.
#
# Development uses Hyp's documented TEST terminal. The production terminal is
# provisioned separately and carries its own monthly cost, so the two
# environments differ by these three values and by nothing in the code.
#
# WHY THERE IS NO WEBHOOK SECRET HERE, and why that is not an omission: this
# tier has no webhook and no standalone transaction-status query. The only
# completion signal is the buyer's browser being redirected back to us, and what
# makes that trustworthy is VERIFY — our server replaying every redirect
# parameter to Hyp, in its original order, and believing only the answer.
# Nothing in the redirect is trusted on its own, and a redirect that fails
# verification writes nothing at all. See .claude/rules/api.md, which amends the
# old webhook-only rule for exactly this reason.
#
# Two settings are NOT variables and live in the Hyp console, at the terminal
# level. The success and failure return URLs are configured there rather than
# sent per transaction. And the payment-page signature must be switched ON only
# AFTER our verification code is built and tested — enabling it early makes every
# test transaction fail with nothing to say why.
HYP_TERMINAL_NUMBER=0000000000
HYP_API_KEY=example-api-key
HYP_API_PASSWORD=example-api-password

# Development only, and normally absent. Points the two Hyp calls at a stand-in
# so the payment return can be exercised before the account exists - there is no
# sandbox host on this provider, and with no credentials the whole return path
# (verify, seize the paintings, mark paid, clear the cart) could otherwise never
# be run even once before a real buyer met it.
#
# It is IGNORED when NODE_ENV is production, which covers the live site and
# Netlify deploy previews both. That guard is the only thing making it safe: an
# override honoured in production would let anyone who can set one environment
# variable answer "this payment is genuine" on our behalf.
#
# Delete it from lib/payments/hyp.js the day there is a test terminal to point
# at. Leave it unset here.
# HYP_ENDPOINT_OVERRIDE=http://127.0.0.1:9500/p/
